---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import VideoRemux from '@/components/tools/media/VideoRemux.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="mov-to-mp4">
  <VideoRemux
    client:load
    from="mov"
    target="mp4"
    hint="QuickTime MOV from an iPhone, a DSLR or a Mac. No queue, and nothing is uploaded"
  />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to convert MOV to MP4</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop your <strong>.mov</strong> file onto the box above, or click it to browse.</li>
      <li>If you want, press <strong>Check what is inside first</strong>. FFmpeg reads the header and shows which codecs the file holds and if they can be copied.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Convert to MP4</strong>. The first run downloads the 10 MB video engine from this site once. After that it is cached and works offline.</li>
      <li>Watch the real percentage, preview the result in the page, and download it.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Why this can finish in seconds</h2>
    <p>
      A video file has two separate parts. The container (MOV, MP4, MKV) is metadata that describes
      where each chunk of data is. The codec (H.264, HEVC, ProRes) is how the pictures are compressed.
      MOV and MP4 are very close relatives: both use the ISO base media file format, which Apple created
      as QuickTime and gave to the standards body that made it into MP4.
    </p>
    <p>
      So when your MOV already contains H.264 or HEVC video and AAC audio (almost all iPhone
      recordings, DSLR clips and QuickTime exports), MOV to MP4 does not change the picture data.
      FFmpeg copies each compressed packet into a new container and writes a new index. That is a
      <strong>remux</strong>, and so a 2 GB file can convert faster than an upload service could
      receive it.
    </p>

    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>What is inside your MOV</th>
            <th>What happens</th>
            <th>Speed</th>
            <th>Quality</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>H.264 + AAC (iPhone, most cameras)</td>
            <td>Streams copied into MP4</td>
            <td>Seconds</td>
            <td>Identical. Nothing re-compressed</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>HEVC / H.265 + AAC (iPhone “High Efficiency”)</td>
            <td>Streams copied into MP4</td>
            <td>Seconds</td>
            <td>Identical, but needs a recent player</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>H.264 + PCM audio (screen capture, some editors)</td>
            <td>Video copied, audio re-encoded to AAC</td>
            <td>Fast</td>
            <td>Picture untouched, audio effectively unchanged</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Apple ProRes / Animation (editing masters)</td>
            <td>Full transcode to H.264</td>
            <td>Around real time</td>
            <td>Much smaller file, some loss</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Windows will not play my iPhone MOV</h2>
    <p>
      This can be two different problems. If the file does not open, the cause is the container:
      Windows Media Player has never handled QuickTime well, and MP4 fixes it. If the file opens but
      shows a black screen or a missing-codec error, the cause is <strong>HEVC</strong>, and Windows
      charges for an HEVC decoder. Then a stream copy is not enough. Set the method to
      <strong>always re-encode</strong>, so the video becomes H.264, which all versions of Windows play
      without extra software.
    </p>
    <p>
      To avoid the problem at the source, on an iPhone set <strong>Settings → Camera → Formats → Most
      Compatible</strong>. It records H.264, not HEVC.
    </p>

    <h2>What this tool does not do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>It does not encode <strong>ProRes</strong>. It can read ProRes MOV files and convert them to H.264, but it cannot make a ProRes master. That needs a desktop encoder.</li>
      <li>It does not edit. Trimming, cropping and rotating are separate tools on this site, so each one does one clear job.</li>
      <li>It does not repair a damaged MOV. If the recording stopped early and the index (the “moov atom”) was never written, no converter can recover it. This one tells you so and does not make a zero-byte file.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Why do it in the browser at all</h2>
    <p>
      An upload converter puts your footage on a rented machine, often with a 100 MB or 1 GB cap and a
      queue, and your video stays on its disk until deletion. Here the same FFmpeg code runs as
      WebAssembly in your tab. The size limit is your own memory, there is no queue, and the file cannot
      leave the device: the site’s content security policy lists no third-party origin.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
